A Profile is now filled from the provider's own property map, the one the control panel edits, rather than from a two-entry constant. `fullname` and `email` were all a provider could ever write onto a Profile, while the map offered every user field and reached the Plone user only — and on a site with the `[profile]` layer the Plone user is served *from* the Profile, so a mapped field that never landed there was configured, applied and invisible.

The map is read per provider and restricted to the fields a provider may own: `fullname`, `email`, `home_page`, `description` and `location`. `userid`, `login` and `group_ids` are never writable whatever a map says — the first two are the join and the case-folded index the enumeration plugin queries, and a provider that could edit the third could grant itself roles. A map naming one of them is ignored rather than refused, because the map is typed into a control panel and a typo there must not fail a login. A provider with no map of its own still gets name and address, which is what an unconfigured provider owes the site.

Claims are addressed by dotted path here as they are everywhere else, so `address.formatted` reaches into a provider's own document. A path landing on an object or a list is treated as an absent claim rather than written as a repr. The ownership rule is unchanged: a provider writes a field only while it still holds the value it last wrote, so a human's edit survives the next login. @ericof
